Umbra MAP Page Pool and Disk Root
=================================

Umbra stores logical-to-physical translations in a relation-local private MAP
fork.  Block 0 is a versioned, CRC-protected relation root.  The remaining
blocks are ordinary MAP pages served by the small shared pool in this
directory.  `ummap.c` owns both disk formats and interprets their contents;
the ordinary page pool owns residency, concurrency, and writeback only for
whole ordinary MAP pages.  The disk root uses direct, full-block I/O in this
patch and is not admitted to the ordinary page pool.

The pool deliberately follows the core ownership and replacement rules of the
PostgreSQL shared buffer pool.  It is not a second general-purpose buffer
manager: it caches only Umbra MAP metadata, not relation data pages.


Tags, Pins, and Content Locks
-----------------------------

Each pool entry is tagged with a RelFileLocatorBackend and a MAP block number.
A partitioned mapping lock protects the tag-to-slot hash table.  A lookup that
finds a tag increments the slot's pin count before releasing that mapping lock.
Once pinned, the slot cannot be recycled for another MAP page.  This is the
same handoff rule used by shared buffers.

A pin protects the slot identity and page memory.  The content lock protects
the page contents.  Callers hold a pin and a shared content lock to inspect
entries, or an exclusive content lock to modify them.  Pins and input I/O
ownership are registered with the current ResourceOwner, so ERROR and
subtransaction cleanup cannot leave a slot pinned or an input I/O owner
behind.

Input publishes a tagged slot as I/O in progress before reading it.  The slot
becomes valid only after the read completes.  A failed input removes the hash
entry and tag before the slot can be reused.  Thus a hash lookup never grants
access to a page whose input failed.


Replacement and Writeback
-------------------------

The pool has a freelist and a clock sweep.  A clock candidate is claimed by
atomically changing its pin count from zero to one before it is returned to a
caller.  A dirty victim is written before its tag can be reused.  The old and
new mapping partitions are locked in partition-number order while a tag is
replaced.

Output holds the I/O and exclusive content locks.  A successful write clears
the dirty state.  An error leaves the page dirty for a later retry and releases
the locks through the normal ERROR path.


MAP Page Format
---------------

An ordinary MAP entry stores the exact physical block for one logical block.
An entry without a mapping is InvalidBlockNumber.  When the MAP fork grows,
every new ordinary MAP page, including an intermediate gap page, is written as
0xFF.  Consequently an all-zero ordinary MAP page is corruption, not an
alternative representation of an unmapped page.


Disk Root Authority
-------------------

The root stores a logical EOF and a physical frontier for each tracked fork.
The logical EOF is a CRC-protected lower-bound hint and may lag canonical MAP
state.  The physical frontier is a monotonic high-water mark; MAIN physical
storage is append-only in this patch.  The root never supplies a physical block
for an individual logical block: the ordinary MAP entry remains the sole
authority for each exact L -> P translation.

During normal execution, smgrnblocks reads a root logical EOF, then scans the
canonical MAP tail from that lower bound to the first InvalidBlockNumber.  The
scan overlays this backend's contiguous pending ranges and waits if it meets a
foreign pending barrier.  It rereads the root after the scan and retries if the
hint changed.  The returned size is the MAP-derived EOF, not the root value.
Thus a deferred or aborted root update costs a longer tail scan but cannot
authorize reuse of a logical block that already has a canonical MAP entry.

A missing MAP entry below a normal root EOF is corruption.  Normal execution
does not infer L -> L from the root.

Recovery does not use the root to decide whether a WAL target exists or which
physical block it names.  It continues to use canonical MAP state, the exact P
recorded in WAL, and record-scoped exact or scratch barriers.  Successful redo
updates the root as an output of replay; recovery scratch never advances it.


Mapping Publication
-------------------

Extension publishes the exact contiguous L -> P run selected by the storage
manager.  Normal publication can fill the current logical EOF or repeat an
existing mapping, but cannot replace a visible mapping.  Redo installs the
exact P recorded in WAL; it never repairs a missing entry by guessing L -> L.
Truncation clears every entry in [new logical EOF, old logical EOF), so a later
extension cannot make an old tail visible again merely by filling its first
entry.

Normal first-born publication has two backend-local stages.  Before WAL, a
pending range owns its ordinary MAP pins and a foreign-visible shared barrier.
After XLogInsertRecord returns an EndRecPtr, the critical publication path can
mark already pinned MAP entries canonical, but it cannot allocate or perform
root I/O.  Once the MAP is canonical, it clears the pending bits, releases the
pins and barrier, and moves the same preallocated node to a backend-local root
update queue.  This state transition allocates no memory.

UmMappingPublicationDone performs work only outside a critical section.  It
first completes MAP publication that required I/O, then drains the root update
queue.  A caller's outer critical section can defer the latter step until the
next safe call or pre-commit.  During that interval the root may lag, while the
canonical MAP remains visible and authoritative.


WAL Ordering
------------

Each descriptor retains the greatest WAL LSN associated with its unflushed
MAP changes.  Before writing a dirty MAP page, the pool calls XLogFlush() for
that LSN.  Successful output clears both the dirty state and the retained LSN;
failed output leaves both available for retry.  Changes that have no WAL
dependency use InvalidXLogRecPtr.

For direct root output, UmMappingPublicationDone first flushes the root update
queue's EndRecPtr without holding a root LWLock.  It then performs the root
read-modify-write.  Thus neither ordinary MAP output nor the root can reach
durable storage ahead of the WAL that selected P.

DELAY_CHKPT_START covers the WAL-to-canonical-MAP publication window.  It is
released when the pending list is empty, even if a root update remains queued.
At that point each dirty MAP page retains its WAL dependency, so checkpoint
writeback performs its own XLogFlush().  A root update is not a checkpoint
barrier because the root is allowed to lag the MAP.

Before WAL insertion, transaction or subtransaction abort can discard a
pending mapping and leave an unreachable physical orphan.  Once a pending
range has an EndRecPtr and its MAP entry is canonical, abort leaves that MAP in
place but may discard its deferred root update.  This matches PostgreSQL
relation-extension behavior: tuple visibility rolls back, storage extension
does not.  The next normal size lookup scans forward from the stale root, and a
later root advance catches up.  Abort cleanup never attempts root I/O.


Root Locking
------------

Direct root reads and writes use the relation-keyed MAP extension lock: shared
for reads and exclusive for bootstrap or read-modify-write.  Root output never
runs while an ordinary MAP descriptor content, I/O, or mapping-partition lock
is held.  MAP publication first releases its descriptor locks, pins, and
barrier; root completion then performs WAL flush, root lock, root I/O, and root
unlock.

Code holding the root/extension lock must not call MapPageBufferRead.  Loading
or extending an ordinary MAP page can acquire the same relation-keyed extension
lock and would self-deadlock.  A normal size lookup releases the root lock
before scanning the ordinary MAP tail, then rereads the root to verify that its
starting hint remained stable.


Checkpoint Start Barrier
------------------------

WAL-backed mapping publication uses PostgreSQL's DELAY_CHKPT_START flag to keep
checkpoint start out of the interval between mapping WAL insertion and
checkpoint-visible MAP state.  If a checkpoint observes the backend before the
flag is set, the mapping WAL has not been inserted and will follow that
checkpoint's redo point.  If it observes the flag, it waits.  After the flag is
cleared, the existing publication path has made the canonical MAP entry visible
and dirty before checkpoint writeback begins.

The barrier does not define or validate the WAL payload, MAP publication, redo,
or root durability protocols.  Those are preconditions owned by their existing
paths.  It also does not wait for MAP fsync; checkpoint writeback and the WAL
flush frontier above own persistence after the MAP state is visible.


Lifecycle
---------

The selected storage manager receives generic checkpoint and database lifecycle
events from core.  Umbra translates them into private MAP operations.  A
checkpoint flushes dirty MAP pages.  Immediate and registered relation syncs
flush matching MAP pages before syncing their relation storage.  Relation,
database, and tablespace invalidation discard matching slots before their files
can be removed or copied.

Logical truncation lowers the root EOF and clears every stale MAP entry in the
truncated range.  MAIN physical storage remains append-only in this patch, so
MAIN truncation does not lower its physical frontier or remove its old physical
tail.  Transaction and subtransaction abort may leave a stale root behind a
canonical MAP prefix.  DROP removes the entire private MAP fork, including its
root.


Deliberate Limits
-----------------

The pool size is fixed at postmaster startup as max(16, NBuffers / 128).  It
has no access-strategy ring, local MAP buffer pool, pin-count waiter, or
background mapwriter.

There is no resident root cache; that is a separate later review unit.  This
patch also does not split next-free allocation from physical capacity, replace
an existing visible mapping, suppress a full-page image, reclaim or reuse
physical blocks, preallocate capacity, run a background mapwriter, or handle
relation generations.  CRC detects an invalid root but does not provide a
duplicate root or repair arbitrary storage corruption.

The lower-bound root contract relies on this patch's append-only physical
allocation and the allocator's check of the actual data-fork EOF.  A later
patch that reclaims or reuses physical blocks must revisit that contract.
